 {"id": "VIS-ALIGN-1", "title": "[P1] the vision channel returns an imaginary scene: 8 ragged rows, structurally 0% against the reader", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": "3", "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "Measured on the live console 2026-10-01 (model openai/gpt-4o-mini, 4.3s, prompt that measured best). Reader RAM and the model do not describe the same screen. RAM says Pallet Town: walls at columns 3-6 of rows 0-1, a doorway and two sprites, mostly open floor. Vision returned 8 rows (not 9), ragged (row 4 is 8 chars), and a completely different place - a sealed B corridor around G tall grass with N, D, T scattered inside. Cell agreement 4/44. Structure agreement 0/22 on the previous pass. Two separate defects are tangled here and must be split: (1) the row count and width are wrong, so the two grids cannot be compared cell for cell at all - a parse or delivery bug, cheap to find; (2) the CONTENT is an invented scene rather than a misread of the real one, which points at the image the model actually receives, not at the prompt or the model. Do NOT shop for a bigger model: syn:large:vision (Kimi K3, 2.8T) scored 62.2% and invented a phantom column, i.e. the same failure at 46x the cost. Next action is to dump the exact PNG bytes handed to the model and look at them - if the image is wrong, no prompt or model will fix this, and one grid/one legend/diff-every-frame depends on it. ROOT CAUSE FOUND 2026-10-01, and it refutes my own filing: the image was never the problem. The frame the console fetches is correct (160x144, 4 greys, 2729 bytes), the x6 upscale is correct (960x864), and the model run OUTSIDE the console with identical inputs reproduced the same junk - so it was never console code either. It is the PROMPT. A/B on one frozen image, same model: the terse bench prompt scored 68.9% agreement with 0% of cells answered \"?\"; the console prompt verbatim scored 36.7% with 41% answered \"?\"; the console prompt with the hedge paragraph removed scored 54.4% with 4%. The clause \"a truthful ? is worth more than a confident wrong character\" made the model obey literally and declare half the screen unknown, then invent a B border to close the shape. My long explanatory prompt was 32 points WORSE than the terse one. FIXED: prompts/vision_10x9.md is now the terse measured winner. Remaining separately: the residual off-by-one column between model and reader, and identifying people in the scene, which deserves its own prompt and its own measurement rather than being bolted onto this one."}
 {"id": "VIS-PEOPLE-1", "title": "[P1] identifying the people in the scene needs its own prompt and its own measurement", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": "3", "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "Bane asked for this explicitly and it is still unbuilt at any price point. Measured on one frame: all three models tested said only the player is present, while the reader RAM marks two other sprite cells. Kimi K3 (2.8T) answered dark hair and a cap facing up toward the doorway - wrong facing, stated confidently. gpt-4o-mini got the facing right but hair and clothing unknown. nova-lite said red hair and red clothing at a cell one off. No model is reliable here yet. Two lessons already paid for, to apply: (1) VIS-ALIGN-1 proved prompt wording moves agreement 30+ points on this exact task, so the people prompt must be terse and measured, not narrated at length; (2) a raw cell-match percentage is gameable by a lazy answer, so the people answer must be scored against the reader sprite cells, never the whole grid. Criteria: a prompt naming each visible person with a cell and a distinguishing feature, scored against the RAM sprite table across a set of frames, with its failure rate reported honestly."}
